Q: How do I apply CSS styles only when an element is focused via a mouse-click-and-hold, and then loses focus when released outside the element? A: <p>To achieve this accessibility design concept, you only need to specify the <code>:focus</code> pseudo-class with specific exclusion of other pseudo-classes, in this case, the <code>:hover</code> and <code>:active</code> pseudo-classes. Here is the complete CSS:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>#submit:focus:not(:hover :active) {
  background-color: red;
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p><em>Update: The previous solution is incomplete. Priority CSS</em> (<em><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Cascade/Introduction" rel="nofollow noreferrer">CSS Cascade</a></em>) <em>and JavaScript overrides are required to achieve the intended output.</em></p>
<p>Here is the full <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/help/minimal-reproducible-example">MRE</a> code:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>(function () {
  const btn = document.getElementById("submit");

  btn.addEventListener("click", (e) =&gt; {
    // or pointerup event type
    btn.classList.add("active");
  });

  btn.addEventListener("mousedown", () =&gt; {
    btn.classList.remove("active");
  });
})();</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>#submit {
  background-color: blue;
}

#submit:hover {
  background-color: green !important;
}

#submit:focus:not(:hover :active) {
  background-color: red;
}

.submit.active {
  background-color: blue !important;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="utf-8" /&gt;
    &lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" /&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;CSS Playground&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;link rel="icon" type="image/x-icon" href="img/www_icon.ico" /&gt;
    &lt;link href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/bootstrap/5.2.3/css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet"/&gt;
    &lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="css/style.css" /&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;

  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;div class="d-flex justify-content-center align-items-center vh-100"&gt;
      &lt;button class="submit text-white fw-bold" id="submit"&gt;Click me&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;script src="./script.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Here are the key reasons why the code snippets above work!</p>
<ul>
<li>Removing the <code>!important</code> rule from the <code>#submit.active</code> selector will break the expected behavior, as <code>#submit:focus:not(:hover, :active)</code> takes precedence.</li>
<li>The <code>mousedown</code> event fires before the <code>click</code> or <code>pointerup</code> events. This is essential for resetting the style properties when the button receives different pointer interactions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Lastly, I tweaked the <strong><code>:hover</code></strong> rule by adding an <strong><code>!important</code></strong> flag to ensure the hover state prevails in the cascade.</p>

Q: What is @[] and @{} in Objective-C? A: <p>First one <strong>@[]</strong> is a shorthand to create an Array, for example the following array of two elements:</p>

<pre><code>NSArray *array = @[ @"First", @"Second"];
</code></pre>

<p>Second one <strong>@{}</strong> creates a dictionary, for example:</p>

<pre><code>NSDictionary *dictionary = @{
    @"first" : someValue,
    @"second" : someValue,
};
</code></pre>
